Found while measuring karr #138 (docs/adr/0042), not fixed there. version is a Go string, so mapstructure weakly stringifies it - and sops then SEMVER-PARSES it and refuses the document when it has no Major.Minor.Patch.

MEASURED, sops 3.13.3, nested YAML sops section, sops -d:

  version: 3.13.3     exit 0
  version: "3.13.3"   exit 0   (and sops rotate writes it back unquoted)
  version: 3          exit 1   "No Major.Minor.Patch elements found"
  version: 3.13       exit 1   same
  version: true       exit 1   same (weakly stringified to "1" first)
  version: []         exit 1   "expected type string, got unconvertible type"

File::SOPS::Metadata->from_hash accepts every one of these and carries them through. That is PERMISSIVE - we read documents sops refuses - so nothing is silently mis-decrypted, but it is still a divergence and the fail-loud rule says a document sops stops on should stop here too.

Deliberately left out of karr #138, whose scope was the two fields that are not strings. Deciding this one needs its own measurement: what sops does on ENCRYPT with a bad version, whether it is checked on every path or only on decrypt, and what a MISSING version does (from_hash defaults it to 3.7.3 today).

Lane: api.
MEASURED (sops 3.13.3, /tmp/sops, one age recipient, 45 version spellings, one document per row).

The ticket's table is confirmed and is INCOMPLETE in three ways that change the answer:

1. AN ABSENT version key is refused too - 'Version string empty', exit 1, same as the empty string and null. from_hash defaults it to 3.7.3, so we invent a version for a document sops stops on.
2. It is refused on EVERY read path, not only -d: 'sops -d --extract' and 'sops rotate' stop with the same message.
3. sops uses MORE of semver than three dotted numbers, and has two rules of its own on top:
   - a leading 'v' is stripped: v3.13.3 exit 0, V3.13.3 and vv3.1.3 exit 1
   - a version whose TEXT begins '1.' is accepted WITHOUT BEING PARSED AT ALL: 1.a.b, 1.2, 1.2.3.4, 1.2.3-01, 1.2.3-! are all exit 0, while 10.2.3-01 and v1.2.3-01 (same suffix, no '1.' prefix) are exit 1. The bypass is tested before the v-strip.
   - everything else is blang/semver strict Parse: 3.13.3-rc.1, 3.13.3+build.5, 3.13.3-alpha.beta+exp.sha.5114f85 exit 0; 03.13.3, 3.13.03, 3.13.3-, 3.13.3+, 3.13.3-!, 3.13.3-01, 3.13.3+a..b, 18446744073709551616.0.0 exit 1.

THE COUNTER-DIRECTION the ticket asked for: we NEVER write a version sops would refuse. policy_args deliberately omits version, so every write path builds a fresh Metadata stamped 3.7.3 and a value read out of a document never reaches a document. This is purely a read question.

AND: sops rotate writes the version back VERBATIM - v3.13.3, 1.2.3, 3.13.3-rc.1, 3.7.3 all come out as they went in. It does not normalise the field to its own version.

DECIDED: not implemented, documented. A faithful refusal is blang/semver's strict grammar plus the two sops quirks above; a partial one ('three dotted numbers') refuses v3.13.3, 3.13.3-rc.1, 3.13.3+build.5 and 1.a.b - every one of which sops writes, reads at exit 0 and preserves across a rotate, i.e. ADR 0038's 'we are wrong' class. Nothing in this distribution reads the field: not in the MAC, not in the AAD, not in any decryption decision.

WHAT DID CHANGE: the one row of the table that is not about semver at all. 'version: []' is 'expected type string, got unconvertible type' at sops and reached our version attribute untouched; it is now refused with the other eight string fields - see karr #146 and docs/adr/0043.

The measured table, the two sops quirks and the absent-version divergence are now in the POD of =attr version and =method from_hash, and pinned in t/57.
